---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  authorName,
  connectionsGameUrl,
  githubUrl,
  openRouterUrl,
  personalSiteUrl,
  puzzleSourceUrl,
} from "../lib/site";

const description =
  "Why benchmark LLMs on Connections, how models play, how to read the results, and known limitations.";

const personJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  name: authorName,
  url: personalSiteUrl || githubUrl,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — llm-connections"
  description={description}
  showNav
  jsonLd={personJsonLd}
>
  <article class="about">
    <h1 class="about__title">About</h1>
    <p class="about__lede">
      A longitudinal benchmark: language models play real{" "}
      <a href={connectionsGameUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">NYT Connections</a> puzzles with the official rules.
      Every guess is logged for comparison, along with the LLM's reasoning.
    </p>

    <section class="about__section">
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        I play Connections almost daily along with other NYT Games. The game is simple with easy to
        describe rules but often tests your creativity and ability to think outside the box.
        I know LLMs know more information than me, as they are training on the totality of human writing.
        Connections is about more than just knowing things though, and I wanted to know whether or not and
        LLM could reliably solve the puzzles.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about__section">
      <h2>Why this is a useful benchmark</h2>
      <p>
        Connections demands <strong>lateral thinking and cultural context</strong> - wordplay,
        idioms, pop and niche knowledge, soft associations. That stresses the breadth and depth of
        a model’s training in a way narrow factual evaluation suite may not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Models also play for real: the same four-by-four board and four-mistake limit, multi-turn
        play (not a one-shot classification), one puzzle at a time, and full
        guess-plus-reasoning logs. Runs go through{" "}
        <a href={openRouterUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenRouter</a> with a shared prompt and
        structured output so results stay comparable across models and dates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about__section">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <ol class="about__steps">
        <li>The model is told how to play and given the current game board.</li>
        <li>
          It guesses a four-word category. The engine replies whether the guess is correct,
          incorrect, or invalid (for example, wrong word count or already-solved words).
        </li>
        <li>
          Play continues until the puzzle is solved or the model runs out of mistakes.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Each turn also records the model’s reasoning, which you can open on any date page’s
        guess-by-guess replay.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about__section">
      <h2>How to read the results</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Season standings</strong> rank models by win rate (solved ÷ completed games),
          then average mistakes. You’ll also see W–L, cost, tokens, and games played.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Mistakes</strong> are wrong category guesses (four lose the game).{" "}
          <strong>Invalid guesses</strong> break the rules and don’t count as mistakes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Date pages</strong> show the board, outcomes per model, and expandable guess
          history with reasoning.
        </li>
        <li>
          The <strong>model filter</strong> defaults to a small curated set; open it to include
          every model that has runs in the archive.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="about__section">
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Unofficial project — not affiliated with The New York Times or Connections.
        </li>
        <li>
          Puzzle archive comes from a third-party source (
          <a href={puzzleSourceUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Eyefyre/NYT-Connections-Answers</a
          >), not NYT directly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Older puzzles may appear in training data; treat win rates as signals, not pure
          generalization proof.
        </li>
        <li>
          The harness does not fully model the human game: it does not reveal category difficulty
          colors when a group is found, and it does not give “one away” hints.
        </li>
        <li>
          Thinking budgets and provider quirks can differ by model even under a shared prompt.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="about__section">
      <h2>Author &amp; links</h2>
      <p>
        Built by {authorName}. Source code is on{" "}
        <a href={githubUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>. Models are called through{" "}
        <a href={openRouterUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenRouter</a>; puzzles from{" "}
        <a href={puzzleSourceUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Eyefyre/NYT-Connections-Answers</a
        >.
        {
          personalSiteUrl && (
            <>
              {" "}
              More at{" "}
              <a href={personalSiteUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">my personal site</a>.
            </>
          )
        }
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
